The additional metadata projection supplies 21,336 SWE-rebench rows from 3,468
repository names and 731 SWE-bench Pro rows from 11 other names. These are
candidate records, not newly solved tasks or confirmed independent families.
No cross-dataset task deduplication or absence of training contamination is
claimed. The exact dataset revisions and Git LFS hashes are frozen in
`additional_metadata_v1/`; all three downloads match their published bytes.

The projector decodes nine SWE-rebench metadata fields and five Pro fields.
Issue bodies, hints, reference patches, test patches, designated test sets and
model quality scores remain undecoded. The raw Parquet files contain those
fields and stay in owner-only curator custody. The whole SWE-rebench test split
was projected; its separately provided filtered split was not used.

SWE-rebench's observed metadata dates range from 2014-04-30 to 2025-04-30.
Only 6,542 of its 21,336 rows have nonempty published image metadata. All 731
Pro rows have image tags; the selected Pro fields supply no task dates. Three
of its eleven repositories are declared Python, with the others declared Go,
JavaScript or TypeScript. These field values are dataset observations, not
independent environment or language qualification.

`rebench_screen_v1/` freezes a further qualification queue before source or
scorer outcomes. It requires metadata dates from 2025 onward, image metadata
and at least two different base commits. It conservatively excludes all
sixteen original and twelve previously cataloged Verified repository names,
and their owner names. The first stage uses one repository per owner and
orders families by a fixed SHA-256 seed. It selects the two latest eligible
metadata rows with different base commits in each family. Fifty-three pairs
meet these rules; sixteen are the primary screening tranche and 37 remain
ordered reserves. Image availability is a convenience filter that may reflect
upstream selection. No treatment/model outcome selected these families.

Native Git checks prove forward base-commit ancestry for all first sixteen
pairs. Each fetch requests `blob:none`; the checker reads commit graphs and
does not open source or patch blobs. A separate current GitHub audit resolves
all sixteen candidates plus 28 prior repositories. The 448 candidate/prior
comparisons share no repository, declared-fork or owner identity. Candidates
also have sixteen distinct current repository and owner IDs, and all current
primary-language fields say Python. The old `ethereum/web3.py` name now
resolves to `ApeWorX/web3.py`; that redirect is retained.

These checks narrow specific identity and ancestry uncertainties. They do not
establish historical derivative independence, shared dependency independence,
portfolio exposure, model-training novelty, source/issue authority, usable
environments or adequate scoring. The metadata date has no timezone suffix;
its upstream event semantics remain unverified. Event ordering in a future
trajectory must follow proved source ancestry. Earlier failed repairs remain
failed when a later upstream snapshot arrives.

All sampled image names explicitly contain `x86_64`. Image metadata alone does
not establish a runnable ARM environment on this host; port or executor
qualification remains necessary. **No additional confirmatory family or
complete natural trajectory is enrolled yet.** The two Requests nodes remain
the current development pair, whose separate environment profiles and failures
are documented in `arm64_qualification/`.

Reproduction, from a fresh revision copy with unused output directories:

```sh
python evals/recruitment/project_additional_metadata.py
python evals/recruitment/plan_rebench_screen.py
python evals/recruitment/check_ancestry.py --output rebench_ancestry_v1 --screen rebench_screen_v1/queue.json --limit 16
python evals/recruitment/audit_repository_identity.py --output rebench_identity_v1 --resume-from repository_identity_v2 --screen rebench_screen_v1/queue.json --limit 16
```

`rebench_screen_audit.json` verifies 175 artifact, selection, identity and
actual-command checks. The source and environment/scorer phase is the next
qualification step; removing difficult families without their prescribed
failure records or treating a reserve as an already qualified task would
invalidate the queue's purpose.
# Replaying the retained screening evidence

The additive `rebench_screen_replay_audit_v2.json` passes 629 checks. Its retained
runner independently reconstructs all 53 eligible pairs and the complete seeded
queue from projected metadata, checks the 16 recorded native ancestry commands,
rechecks captured API-response hashes, and recomputes all 448 current
candidate/prior identity comparisons. It performs no network or model calls and
does not decode protected task fields. The original 175-check audit remains
unchanged.

Reproduce into an unused output path:

```sh
python3 papers/revisions/neurosymbolic_system_scope_20260914/evals/recruitment/replay_rebench_screen_audit.py --output fresh_screen_audit.json
```
